{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And you shall sanctify them.</b> This anointing sanctifies them to be most holy. And what is their sanctity? <b>Whatever touches</b> etc.: everything fit for a service vessel — once it enters into them, it is holy with the sanctity of the body, to disqualify by outgoing and overnight stay and tevul yom, and it is not redeemed to go out to profane use; but something unfit for them does not sanctify. And this is a complete Mishnah taught concerning the altar: Since it is written ״כָּל הַנֹּגֵעַ בַּמִּזְבֵּחַ יִקְדָּשׁ״ (Exodus 29), I would hear whether fit or unfit; therefore Scripture says ״כְּבָשִׂים״ — just as lambs are fit, so all must be fit. All anointings of the Tabernacle and priests and kings are translated as an expression of plurality, because the need for their anointing is only for greatness — for so the King established that this is the inauguration of their greatness. And the rest of the anointings — as in ״רְקִיקִין מְשׁוּחִין״ (ibid.), and ״רֵאשִׁית שְׁמָנִים יִמְשָׁחוּ״ (Amos 6) — Aramaic has in them as in Hebrew."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:55:53.819051146Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}